 Pico text "\n\t\t\t\t"editor.  There are four main sections of the editor.  "\n\t\t\t\t"The top line shows the program version, the current "\n\t\t\t\t"filename being edited, and whether or not the file "\n\t\t\t\t"has been modified.  Next is the main editor window "\n\t\t\t\t"showing the file being edited.  The status line is "\n\t\t\t\t"the third line from the bottom and shows important "\n\t\t\t\t"messages.  ', 932), ('Main nano help text\n\n ', 933), ('The nano editor is designed to emulate the ', 934), ('functionality and ease-of-use of the UW Pico text ', 935), ('editor.  There are four main sections of the editor.  ', 936), ('The top line shows the program version, the current ', 937), ('filename being edited, and whether or not the file ', 938), ('has been modified.  Next is the main editor window ', 939), ('showing the file being edited.  The status line is ', 940), ('the third line from the bottom and shows important ', 941), ('messages.  ', 942), ('The bottom two lines show the most commonly used "\n\t\t\t\t"shortcuts in the editor.\n\n Shortcuts are written as "\n\t\t\t\t"follows: Control-key sequences are notated with a \'^\' "\n\t\t\t\t"and can be entered either by using the Ctrl key or "\n\t\t\t\t"pressing the Esc key twice.  Meta-key sequences are "\n\t\t\t\t"notated with \'M-\' and can be entered using either the "\n\t\t\t\t"Alt, Cmd, or Esc key, depending on your keyboard setup.  ', 943), ('The bottom two lines show the most commonly used ', 944), ('shortcuts in the editor.\n\n Shortcuts are written as ', 945), ("follows: Control-key sequences are notated with a '^' ", 946), ('and can be entered either by using the Ctrl key or ', 947), ('pressing the Esc key twice.  Meta-key sequences are ', 948), ("notated with 'M-' and can be entered using either the ", 949), ('Alt, Cmd, or Esc key, depending on your keyboard setup.  ', 950), ('Also, pressing Esc twice and then typing a "\n\t\t\t\t"three-digit decimal number from 000 to 255 will enter "\n\t\t\t\